Model Comparing_retirement
Title: Comparing retirement account types
Description: You've decided to put away some money this year for retirement. Is it better to put it into a tax-advantaged retirement plan (i.e., IRA or 401(k)) or just a normal brokerage account? Will a Roth IRA make you more than a traditional IRA or 401(k) plan? This model helps you compare the financial return on investment between the different options for your specific situation. The best option for you is not going to be the same for everyone else, and hence a model can help.~
~
For most people, the long-term capital gains tax rate is much lower than the income tax rate. As your investments increase in value, you pay capital gains taxes in a brokerage account, but income tax on those gains in a traditional IRA or 401(k), so from that point of view it would seem that a standard brokerage account is better. But, you if you trade often in the brokerage account, you lose out on the compounding on the money paid in capital gains taxes. Hence, there is a trade-off, and that balance determines the relative merit for you.~
~
The future is uncertain. Market performance is hard to predict, tax laws could change, etc. This model is intended to help you gain insight. You should modify it to incorporate other important factors that impact you, and to change estimates to your own. ~
~
There are many non-financial considerations not considered by this model that may also make a particular type of retirement vehicle a better option for you. For example, the ability to access the savings before retirement without an early withdrawl penalty may be undesirable for some people who would not be able to restrain from spending it before retirement. Retirement accounts may be excluded or discounted when your children apply for college financial aid. If you are sued, a tax-advantaged retirement account may enjoy special protected status, etc. Your should consult other sources to understand those considerations and how they might apply to your situation.
Author: Lonnie Chrisman, Ph.D.~
Lumina Decision Systems
