Decision Account_type
{!40000|Att_PrevIndexValue: ['Brokerage','Roth IRA','Traditional IRA','401k']}
Title: Account type
Description: A brokerage account is a standard account with no special tax treatment. Post-tax wages are deposited, and gains are subject to capital gains taxes whenever investments are sold. No income tax is due on realized gains at retirement.~
~
A Roth IRA allows intermediate gains to compound without having to pay capital gains tax, and there is no income tax due at retirement when you withdraw the funds. You deposit post-tax wages -- i.e., you must pay income tax on the wages before depositing your initial investment.~
~
A traditional IRA and 401(k) work in essentially the same way as far as this analysis is concerned. Per-tax dollars are deposited, and intermediate gains compound without gain taxes, but you are responsible for income tax when funds are withdrawn during retirement. Maintenance fees charged by financial institutions are typically a bit higher for 401(k) plans.
Definition: Choice(Self,0)
