NumberFormat: 2,%,4,4,0,0,4,0,$,0,"ABBREV",0,,,0,0,15

Decision Trading_turnover
Title: Trading turnover
Units: %
Description: The trading turnover characterizes how often you trade investments (sell one and buy another). If you intend to buy stock with your initial investment and then forget about it,  holding the same stock until retirement, then your turnover would be 0%. If you trade actively, so that you will never hold an investment for more than a year, than your turnover would be 100% (and in this case, would be 100% short-term, 0% long-term).~
~
More generally, you'll probably rebalance or otherwise trade some fraction of your portfolio each year. Some fraction of the investments sold will be held for less than a year (short-term), and some fraction will have been held for more than a year (long-term). So you should allocate a percentage to each time, so that the sum reflects the total turnover, and the relative amount reflects the balance between short and long term sales.~
~
This input impacts only the performance of a standard brokerage account, and doesn't alter the overall performance of IRAs or 401(k) accounts. So you should estimate your behavior for a standard brokerage (for example, you might be biased to hold investments long-term in a standard brokerage, but make more short-term trades in your retirement accounts -- if so, this should reflect your brokerage account behavior).
Definition: 20%
